{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's defense relies on 'verisimilitude' (truth-likeness) to justify preferring better-corroborated theories without induction. However, Popper's own formal attempt to define verisimilitude logically failed because it inadvertently reintroduced probabilistic assumptions or required inductive elements to compare the 'distance from truth' between false theories. By invoking verisimilitude as the grounding for rational preference, the target smuggles in the very inductive logic it claims to avoid, failing to answer how the preference is defensible strictly on non-inductive terms.","premise":"Popper's formal definition of verisimilitude was widely criticized (e.g., by Tichý and Miller) for being logically incoherent without relying on probabilistic or inductive assumptions about the content of theories.","successor_question":"If verisimilitude cannot be formally defined without induction, what non-inductive metric does Popper actually offer to rationally distinguish between two equally falsified but differently corroborated theories?","target":"2b2dc8431b18b8aa0541623ac92ad4c34ba8c1437a0d46079e56d4f73cfd40a0"}]}